SpottyTube - install guide
==========================

This walks through a first install on Linux. It assumes you can open a terminal
and paste a command. Nothing here needs root.

You need:

  - Google Chrome, Brave, or Chromium (any of them, already working)
  - Python 3.9 or newer        check with: python3 --version
  - ffmpeg                     check with: ffmpeg -version

If one of those is missing on Ubuntu or Debian:

  sudo apt install python3 ffmpeg

Step 1 - get the folder
-----------------------

Put this project anywhere in your home folder. The examples use:

  ~/Documents/git/spottytube-ext

If you downloaded a ZIP instead of cloning, unzip it and move the folder so the
path matches, or just remember your real path: everything below works with any
path, you only have to type yours instead.

Step 2 - run the installer
--------------------------

  cd ~/Documents/git/spottytube-ext
  ./install.sh

If you get "Permission denied", the file lost its executable bit (this happens
after unzipping on some systems). Fix it once:

  chmod +x install.sh install-chrome.sh

What the installer does, in plain words:

  - links a small command called spottytube-bridge into ~/.local/bin
  - downloads one Python file the tool needs (tools/wav_builder.py)
  - writes a user service so the bridge starts with your session
  - starts it and checks that it answers

You should end with three lines like:

  wrote   ~/.config/systemd/user/spottytube-bridge.service
  started spottytube-bridge on port 8765
  health: bridge answering

Running ./install.sh again later is safe. It rewrites the same files.

Step 3 - load the extension in your browser
-------------------------------------------

The installer prints the exact lines for the browsers it found. In short:

  Chrome or Chromium
    1. type chrome://extensions in the address bar, press Enter
    2. turn on "Developer mode" (top right)
    3. click "Load unpacked"
    4. choose the "extension" folder inside this project

  Brave
    1. type brave://extensions in the address bar, press Enter
    2. turn on "Developer mode" (top right)
    3. click "Load unpacked"
    4. choose the "extension" folder inside this project

Choose the folder named extension, not the project folder above it. The
extension folder is the one that contains manifest.json.

Step 4 - check that it works
----------------------------

Open any video page. A small SpottyTube panel appears in the bottom right.

  green dot   the bridge is up and something can take a sample
  red dot     no bridge, or no instrument and no card to write to

If the panel does not appear, reload the page once. The widget is added when a
page loads, so it is not there on pages that were already open.

Step 5 - pick your browser (optional)
-------------------------------------

Plain ./install.sh handles whichever browsers it finds. If you want to be
explicit:

  ./install-chrome.sh              same as: ./install.sh --browser chrome
  ./install.sh --browser brave
  ./install.sh --browser chromium
  ./install.sh --browser chrome,brave

If you ask for a browser that is not installed, the installer says so and still
sets up the bridge, because the bridge does not care which browser you use.

Flatpak browsers (a normal install from Flathub, not a .deb)
------------------------------------------------------------

A Flatpak browser runs in a sandbox and cannot see your home folder, so
"Load unpacked" silently does nothing. The installer grants read-only access to
this project folder for every Flatpak browser it detects. It prints:

  flatpak: granted Google Chrome read-only access to /path/to/spottytube-ext

Close the browser completely and open it again after the first install. That is
the step people miss.

Snap browsers work as-is; nothing extra to do.

Common problems
---------------

"flatpak: command not found" - harmless. It only means you have no Flatpak
browsers, and the installer skips that part.

"warning: no chrome browser on PATH" - Chrome is not installed, or it is
installed in a way that does not put google-chrome on your PATH. Install it
normally (deb/rpm from Google) or use a different browser. The bridge is set up
regardless.

The extension loads but the panel shows a red dot - the bridge or the instrument
is not reachable. Check the bridge:

  systemctl --user status spottytube-bridge
  curl http://127.0.0.1:8765/status

/status names what it is missing (usb, card). Plug the instrument in over USB,
or mount its card, and the panel turns green within 20 seconds.

Changing the port
-----------------

The extension expects port 8765. If you must change it:

  ./install.sh --port 8766

then edit two places to match: extension/manifest.json (host_permissions) and
BASE in extension/background.js. Reload the extension afterwards.

Removing everything
-------------------

  ./install.sh --uninstall

That stops and deletes the service and the command link. The extension stays in
your browser until you remove it yourself at chrome://extensions or
brave://extensions. The project folder is untouched; delete it whenever you
like.

Where things live
-----------------

  ~/.config/systemd/user/spottytube-bridge.service   the service
  ~/.local/bin/spottytube-bridge                     the command
  journalctl --user -u spottytube-bridge -f          live log

The longer story, including the widget controls, the slot order and the bridge
API, is in README.md.
